From 9ec7e1e17b4fb85f710fee659d507d9bdea2d7b3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Christoph Engelbert (noctarius)" <chris@simplyblock.io>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 10:32:31 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] nfsd: never wait for a layout break with an nfsd thread held

A write through the metadata server, a size change, and an fallocate
each make the filesystem break the pNFS layouts other clients hold on
the file. XFS does that in xfs_break_leased_layouts() and, while layouts
are out, waits for them with the calling nfsd thread held. A client
gives a layout back with LAYOUTRETURN, which itself needs an nfsd
thread. Once every thread waits in __break_lease, no LAYOUTRETURN is
served: nfsd re-sends each recall until two lease periods have passed
and then fences the client, and nothing else is served meanwhile.

Seen on a pNFS metadata server with 8 threads: all of them in
__break_lease for minutes,

    __break_lease
    xfs_break_leased_layouts
    xfs_break_layouts
    xfs_file_write_checks
    xfs_file_buffered_write
    nfsd_vfs_write
    nfsd4_write

while clients reconnected every 30 s and their connections sat in
CLOSE_WAIT on the server.

Start the break without waiting before handing the operation to the
filesystem, and answer NFS4ERR_DELAY (or NFS3ERR_JUKEBOX) while layouts
are still out, as nfsd_open_break_lease() already does for delegations.
The thread is free to take the LAYOUTRETURN, and the client's retry
finds the layouts gone. A layout granted between the check and the
write is still waited for by the filesystem, so the window narrows
rather than closes.
---
 fs/nfsd/vfs.c | 29 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 29 insertions(+)

diff --git a/fs/nfsd/vfs.c b/fs/nfsd/vfs.c
index 53b55ee..b148bc0 100644
--- a/fs/nfsd/vfs.c
+++ b/fs/nfsd/vfs.c
@@ -554,6 +554,20 @@ nfsd_setattr(struct svc_rqst *rqstp, struct svc_fh *fhp,
 
 	nfsd_sanitize_attrs(inode, iap);
 
+	/*
+	 * A size change breaks the pNFS layouts on the file, and the
+	 * filesystem would wait for them with this thread held. The clients
+	 * return them with LAYOUTRETURN, which needs a free nfsd thread, so
+	 * enough such waits stop nfsd until each recall times out and its
+	 * client is fenced. Start the break without waiting and have the
+	 * client retry while layouts are still out.
+	 */
+	if (size_change) {
+		host_err = break_layout(inode, false);
+		if (host_err)
+			goto out;
+	}
+
 	/*
 	 * The size case is special, it changes the file in addition to the
 	 * attributes, and file systems don't expect it to be mixed with
@@ -745,6 +759,11 @@ __be32 nfsd4_vfs_fallocate(struct svc_rqst *rqstp, struct svc_fh *fhp,
 	if (!S_ISREG(file_inode(file)->i_mode))
 		return nfserr_inval;
 
+	/* Breaks layouts like a write does: see nfsd_vfs_write. */
+	error = break_layout(file_inode(file), false);
+	if (error)
+		return nfserrno(error);
+
 	error = vfs_fallocate(file, flags, offset, len);
 	if (!error)
 		error = commit_metadata(fhp);
@@ -1233,6 +1252,16 @@ nfsd_vfs_write(struct svc_rqst *rqstp, struct svc_fh *fhp,
 
 	exp = fhp->fh_export;
 
+	/*
+	 * A write through the metadata server breaks the pNFS layouts other
+	 * clients hold on the file, which the filesystem would wait for with
+	 * this thread held. See nfsd_setattr: start the break without waiting
+	 * and have the client retry while layouts are still out.
+	 */
+	host_err = break_layout(file_inode(file), false);
+	if (host_err)
+		goto out_nfserr;
+
 	if (!EX_ISSYNC(exp))
 		stable = NFS_UNSTABLE;
 	init_sync_kiocb(&kiocb, file);
